=== QE001 | an unclosed brace
f:{

=== QE001 | an unclosed bracket
f:{[a] a[}

=== QE001 | an unterminated string
s:"abc

=== QE001 profile: general | a line at column 0 ends a string, though a quote closes it there (q 5: '")
s:"abc
def"
t:1

=== QE001 profile: general | in a lambda too (q 5: '{)
f:{s:"abc
def";s}

=== clean profile: general | an indented or blank line continues a string (q 5: "abc\n\n def")
s:"abc

 def"

=== clean | a brace inside a string is not a delimiter
s:"{[desc]}"

=== clean | a bracket inside a comment is not a delimiter
x:1 / (]

=== clean | balanced nesting
f:{[a] $[a>0;(1;2);()]}

=== QE001 profile: general | a closing brace at column 0 starts a new statement (q 5: '{)
f:{[y]
  y+1
}

=== QE001 profile: general | so does any line at column 0 inside a bracket (q 5: '()
a:(1;
2)

=== clean profile: general | indented closers, and a column-0 comment inside a lambda (q 5: f 1 is 2)
f:{[y]
/ a note
  y+1
 }
a:(1;
 2)

=== QE002 | an escape q does not define
s:"a\qb"

=== clean | q 5 takes \/ as a slash ("a\/b" is "a/b")
s:"a\/b"

=== clean | the escapes q does define
s:"a\tb\nc\\d\"e"

=== clean | a backslash outside a string is not an escape
\d .ns
\d .

=== QE003 | a block comment that nothing closes swallows the file
/
anything after is comment

=== clean | a closed block comment is only QP001's concern, not a syntax error
/
inside
\

=== QT003 profile: general | block comments nest, so the inner backslash leaves f commented (q 5 loads g alone)
/
/
\
f:1+`a
\
g:1+`a

=== QE003 | a nested block left open swallows the file
/
/
\
f:1+`a

=== QT003 profile: general | a slash with text after it is a comment line and nests nothing
/
/ x
\
g:1+`a

=== QE003 | the closer has to be a bare backslash, not a directive
/
text
\d .

=== QE004 | double equals is not q
if[a==1;2]

=== QE004 | bang-equals is not q either
if[a!=1;2]

=== QE004 | and-and
r:a&&b

=== QE004 | plus-equals
a+=1

=== clean | a string and a comment on a \t line are masked (q 5 runs both: 0 and 0)
\t count "a==b"
\t 1+1 / x==y
\ts:2 count "12:60"

=== QE004 | the expression of a \t line is still q
\t x==y

=== clean | another command's argument is no q (q 5 loads \l inc.q / x==y and \d .foo / a==b)
\l inc.q / x==y
\d .foo / a==b
\d .
\echo "a==b"

=== clean | an indented line after a command is the command's argument (q 5 loads this, ctx .foo)
a:0
\d .foo
  x==y
\d .

=== QE004 | an indented line after \t is part of its expression
a:0
\t 1+
  x==y

=== clean | the q spellings
if[a=1;2];if[a<>1;2];r:a and b;a+:1;if[a>=1;2]

=== clean | an = applied with brackets is its own token (q 5: c:=[`a;`b] is 0b, x+=[1;1] and x==[1;1] parse)
c:=[`a;`b]
d:= [1;1]
e:x+=[1;1]
f:x==[1;1]

=== clean | a second glyph derived by an iterator and applied with brackets (q 5: x||/[y] is 4 4, x**/[y] 12 24)
x:1 2;y:3 4
a:x||/[y]
b:x**/[y]
c:x&&/[y]
d:x==/[y]
e:x:=/[y]
f:x->/[y]
g:x||':[y]
h:x**\[y]

=== QE004 | without the brackets the pair is refused (q 5: x:=1 and x:=(1;1) are '=)
a:=1
b:=(1;1)

=== clean | operators inside a string are text
s:"a==b"

=== QD001 | til of a negative count
r:til[-1]

=== QD002 | where with negative repetition counts
r:where[-1 2]

=== clean | non-negative counts
r:where[1 2]

=== QF017 | a string continued inside a lambda leaves the code after it outside the string
f:{s:"abc
  def"; +\[x]}

=== QE002 QF017 | while a bad escape on the string's second line is still found
f:{s:"abc
  d\ef"; x}

=== clean | a t) line belongs to the language .t.e defines, as p) and k) do
t)"Hello"~r
t)abs[x]<tol

=== clean | after a number `_` is drop, so `3_ns` reads `ns`
f:{[x] ns:x+1; 3_ns}

=== QF017 | after a letter `_` joins the name: `ns_3` is another name
f:{[x] ns:x+1; ns_3:2; ns_3}

=== QE004 profile: general | the longer spellings from other languages (q 5 parse refuses each)
a:x**2
b:x=>y
c:x=<y
d:x<<2
e:x|>f
f:x->y
x:=1
g:x%%y
h:x<=>y
k:x+++y

=== clean profile: general | what parses: a trailing ++ or ** is a projection, and x>>2 runs
a:a++
b:a**
c:x>>2
d:x<-1
